{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_80","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_11-29-07-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('submissions/404d2b52df234f37ba0839b3a65db627/raw.json'))\nprint('top keys:', list(d.keys()))\nc=d['candidate']\nprint('candidate keys:', list(c.keys()))\nprint()\nprint('QUESTION:', json.dumps(c.get('question'), indent=1)[:2500])\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"top keys: ['action', 'rationale', 'candidate', 'plan_path', 'harness_path']\ncandidate keys: ['action', 'rationale', 'question', 'obligation', 'bindings']\n\nQUESTION: {\n \"question\": \"Can a leader acknowledge a client write at a log index where the other voters of the configuration store a different entry, i.e. can a reported commit index name an entry that the configuration does not hold?\",\n \"importance\": \"A client write that is acknowledged must be the entry the configuration stored: the whole purpose of committing by quorum is that the acknowledged value survives and that every replica applies the same commands in the same order. If a leader can count a quorum for a command the other voter never applied, the acknowledgement is false - the client acts on a value that no other replica holds - and the log positions of the two leaders diverge with the same index and term, so any later read, leader change or recovery can surface a different history for the same committed position. The enabling chain (two leaders of one term, one of which proposed a term it had already voted in after its published term dropped) was confirmed separately, so the remaining question is what the acknowledgement actually guarantees.\",\n \"trigger_rationale\": \"A follower that already holds an entry at the same index compares only terms: when the terms match it treats the incoming entry as a duplicate and stores nothing, then reports success, so the leader records the voter's match at that index and its commitment reaches a majority. With two leaders of one term writing different entries at the same index, that reply tells the leader the position is written without asserting that the same command is written there, and the client's future completes without error.\",\n \"disposition\": \"ready_for_check\",\n \"preferred_check\": \"direct_test\",\n \"participants\": [\n  \"the two leaders of one term\",\n  \"the voter that acknowledges both\",\n  \"the client whose write is acknowledged\"\n ],\n \"objects\": [\n  \"commitment.matchIndexes and commitIndex\",\n  \"the log entry at one index on two voters\",\n  \"the ApplyFuture the client holds\",\n  \"the FSM applied values of both voters\"\n ],\n \"contexts\": [\n  \"three voters with two leaders of the same term that cannot reach each other but both reach the third\",\n  \"a client write issued to one of the two leaders\"\n ],\n \"event_paths\": [\n  \"leader path: Apply appends the command at the next index, replicates it to the reachable voter and commits when the tracked match index reaches a majority\",\n  \"voter path: the reachable voter already holds an entry at that index with the same term, so the conflict loop treats the incoming entry as a duplicat\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
